(
  'bbbbbbbb-bbbb-4bbb-8bbb-bbbbbbbbbbbb',
  'blog',
  'vegetarian-turkish-food-guide',
  'Vegetarian Turkish Food: A Practical Ordering Guide',
  'Explore the vegetables, legumes, grains, yogurt, bread and meze that can shape a vegetarian Turkish meal.',
  '[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Vegetarian Turkish food is best understood through ingredients and preparation rather than a short list of fixed dishes. Turkish cooking uses vegetables, legumes, grains, yogurt, cheese, herbs, olive oil, nuts and bread in many combinations. A table can feel varied and complete without centering every course on meat, but recipes differ by region and restaurant.</p><p>This guide offers a framework for ordering. It is not a dietary guarantee. Broths, garnishes, shared cooking surfaces and recipe details can change, so guests should always confirm current ingredients directly with the restaurant.</p><h2>Build the table around contrasting meze</h2><p>Cold and warm meze make an adaptable beginning. Look for contrasts such as creamy yogurt beside bright herbs, smoky eggplant beside fresh tomato, or a smooth legume spread beside crisp vegetables. Hummus, vegetable salads and olive-oil preparations may fit a vegetarian meal, depending on each recipe.</p><p>Two or three meze are often enough for a pair of diners to begin exploring. A larger group can choose more variety, but ordering in stages leaves room for warm food. Bread adds texture and makes it easier to compare each preparation without mixing every flavor at once.</p><h2>Add grains, legumes and warm vegetables</h2><p>Rice, bulgur, lentils and beans appear throughout Turkish cooking. They may be served as accompaniments, soups, salads or more substantial preparations. Stuffed vegetables, roasted vegetables and oven-baked dishes can add warmth and depth. Ask whether stock, meat or dairy is used when those ingredients matter to your diet.</p><h3>Understand cheese, yogurt and pastry</h3><p>Vegetarian does not always mean vegan. Yogurt, butter, cheese and eggs can appear in meze, pastries, sauces and breads. Guests avoiding dairy or eggs should ask about each component, including dough and garnish. A vegetable name in a menu description does not confirm how the entire dish is prepared.</p><h2>Create a satisfying progression</h2><p>Begin with fresh and cool dishes, continue with something warm or baked, then add a grain, salad or vegetable plate if the table needs more substance. Dessert and tea can provide a clear final course. This progression creates variety through temperature, texture and cooking method rather than simply increasing quantity.</p><p>Review the <a href=\"/menu\">current Turquaz menu</a> before visiting because selections and prices may change. When dietary restrictions are important, contact the restaurant and describe them clearly. For a planned meal with friends or family, <a href=\"/#reservation\">reserve a table</a> and allow time to ask questions before ordering.</p>"}]',
  'published',
  'Vegetarian Turkish Food Ordering Guide | Turquaz',
  'Explore vegetarian Turkish food through meze, vegetables, grains, legumes, yogurt and bread, with practical ordering guidance from Turquaz.',
  'https://www.turquazsf.com/assets/images/gallery-1.jpeg',
  'Colorful vegetable-forward Turkish dishes and meze arranged for sharing',
  'vegetarian Turkish food',
  'Turquaz',
  '2026-08-27T23:50:00.000Z',
  '2026-08-27T23:50:00.000Z',
  '2026-08-27T23:50:00.000Z'
),
(
  'cccccccc-cccc-4ccc-8ccc-cccccccccccc',
  'blog',
  'turkish-herbs-spices-guide',
  'Turkish Herbs and Spices: A Guide to Flavor',
  'Learn how pepper, sumac, cumin, mint, parsley, dill and other seasonings create balance in Turkish cooking.',
  '[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Turkish herbs and spices build flavor through balance rather than heat alone. A dish may combine a warm spice with fresh herbs, acidity, olive oil or yogurt. The same ingredient can taste different when toasted, ground, infused into oil or added fresh at the table.</p><p>Turkey includes many regional cuisines, so there is no single seasoning formula for every kebab, soup, meze or pastry. The most useful approach is to notice what each ingredient contributes and how it supports the main food.</p><h2>Pepper brings warmth, fruit and color</h2><p>Different dried peppers can contribute gentle warmth, deeper heat, sweetness or a fruity quality. Pul biber is a broad term often associated with crushed red pepper flakes, but its exact character and intensity vary. Pepper pastes can also add concentrated flavor and color to marinades, spreads and cooked dishes.</p><p>Guests who are sensitive to spice should ask about the current recipe. A red dish is not necessarily very hot, and a mild-looking preparation can still contain pepper. Tasting a small amount first is more reliable than judging by color.</p><h2>Sumac and lemon provide brightness</h2><p>Ground sumac is known for a tart, fruity character. It may appear on onions, salads, grilled food or at the table as a finishing seasoning. Lemon performs a related but distinct role, bringing fresh acidity and moisture. Both can keep rich foods lively and sharpen the flavor of vegetables and herbs.</p><h2>Cumin adds an earthy foundation</h2><p>Cumin can give legumes, meat mixtures and warm dishes an earthy, savory depth. It is assertive, so it often works as one part of a blend rather than the only flavor. Black pepper, coriander and other spices may join it according to the dish and regional tradition.</p><h3>Fresh and dried herbs behave differently</h3><p>Parsley, dill and mint can bring freshness to salads, fillings, yogurt preparations and garnishes. Dried mint has a more concentrated profile and is often useful in soups or sauces. Oregano and thyme may contribute herbal depth to vegetables, grilled food or olive-oil dishes. Fresh herbs can brighten the final plate, while dried herbs have more time to blend into a cooked preparation.</p><h2>How to taste seasonings at the table</h2><p>Try a dish before adding extra seasoning. Then notice whether it needs acidity, warmth or a cooling contrast. Yogurt can soften spice; bread and grains can reset the palate; pickled or fresh vegetables can provide brightness between richer bites.</p><p>Use the <a href=\"/menu\">Turquaz menu</a> to see current dishes, then ask the team which seasonings define a preparation. Ingredient questions should be specific when allergies or sensitivities are involved. To explore several dishes side by side, <a href=\"/#reservation\">reserve a table</a> and build a shared order with different cooking methods.</p>"}]',
  'published',
  'Turkish Herbs and Spices Guide | Turquaz',
  'Learn how Turkish herbs and spices such as pepper, sumac, cumin, mint, parsley and dill create warmth, brightness and balance in cooking.',
  'https://www.turquazsf.com/assets/images/dish-feature.jpeg',
  'Turkish dish finished with colorful herbs and spices at Turquaz',
  'Turkish herbs and spices',
  'Turquaz',
  '2026-08-27T23:51:00.000Z',
  '2026-08-27T23:51:00.000Z',
  '2026-08-27T23:51:00.000Z'
),
(
  'dddddddd-dddd-4ddd-8ddd-dddddddddddd',
  'blog',
  'family-style-turkish-dinner',
  'Family-Style Turkish Dinner: How to Plan the Table',
  'Plan a shared Turkish dinner with a practical sequence of meze, bread, warm dishes, sides, dessert and tea.',
  '[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A family-style Turkish dinner gives everyone a chance to taste, compare and talk as the meal develops. The goal is not to cover the table with every dish at once. A better plan creates a sequence: a few opening plates, warm food with contrasting sides, and a final drink or dessert.</p><p>Sharing works for many group sizes, but the order should reflect appetites, timing and dietary needs. A table of four needs a different quantity from a party of twelve. Current portions and availability are restaurant-specific, so use this guide as a planning framework and confirm details with the team.</p><h2>Start with a focused meze selection</h2><p>Choose a small set of meze with different textures and flavor profiles. A creamy dish, a vegetable preparation and something bright or spicy can create enough contrast. Bread helps guests move between plates, but it should support the opening course rather than fill everyone before warm dishes arrive.</p><p>Ask the group about allergies and dietary restrictions before ordering. Yogurt, sesame, nuts, wheat and other allergens may appear in sauces, bread, pastries or garnish. Recipe names alone do not provide enough information for allergy decisions.</p><h2>Choose warm dishes that complement one another</h2><p>A shared main course can include a grilled preparation, an oven-baked dish, vegetables, salad and a grain. Select different cooking methods instead of several plates with the same texture. For example, a baked bread dish can contrast with grilled food and a fresh salad.</p><h3>Decide what should be individual</h3><p>Not every item has to be shared. A guest may prefer an individual bowl or plate because of dietary needs or personal taste. Drinks are also easiest to order individually. Family-style dining should make the meal flexible, not pressure every person to eat the same things.</p><h2>Order in stages and protect the pace</h2><p>Beginning with a moderate order lets the table see portion sizes and decide what it still needs. Ask whether warm dishes will arrive together or in stages. If the group has a deadline, such as a performance or meeting, explain that timing early so the restaurant can describe realistic options.</p><p>For larger groups, choose one person to coordinate questions and confirm the final order. This reduces repeated requests while still giving every guest a chance to state dietary needs.</p><h2>Finish the gathering deliberately</h2><p>Turkish coffee, tea and dessert can turn the end of dinner into its own course. One or two sweets may be enough for a table that wants a taste. Coffee offers a concentrated finish, while tea can extend conversation more gently.</p><p>Browse the <a href=\"/menu\">current menu</a> before choosing a framework. For a family-style Turkish dinner at a specific time, <a href=\"/#reservation\">make a reservation</a>. Contact the restaurant directly for parties larger than the online reservation limit or when the gathering needs special arrangements.</p>"}]',
  'published',
  'Family-Style Turkish Dinner Guide | Turquaz',
  'Plan a family-style Turkish dinner with meze, bread, warm dishes, vegetables, dessert and tea, including practical guidance for groups.',
  'https://www.turquazsf.com/assets/images/dish-feast.jpeg',
  'Family-style Turkish dinner with shared meze, bread and warm dishes',
  'family-style Turkish dinner',
  'Turquaz',
  '2026-08-27T23:52:00.000Z',
  '2026-08-27T23:52:00.000Z',
  '2026-08-27T23:52:00.000Z'
